#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059511 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bba0ad98-6940-502a-873c-8a589a03a499" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Coterminal Angles in Degrees"}] :question/id #uuid "fae68ee3-73e6-53a1-a141-e362b8118964" :question/math-academy-id "e-3839" :question/problem "Which angles are coterminal with $240^\\circ$?\n\n$600^\\circ, \\quad 300^\\circ, \\quad -480^\\circ$." :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta$, we add and subtract integer multiples of $360^\\circ\\mathbin{.}$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $360^\\circ$ to $240^\\circ$:\n\n$$\n240^{∘} + 360^{∘} = 600^{∘}✓\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than $600^\\circ$, so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $360^\\circ$ from $240^\\circ$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n240^{∘} - 360^{∘} &= -120^{∘} \\\\\n240^{∘} - 2 \\cdot (360^{∘}) &= -480^{∘}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-480^\\circ$, so we can stop here.\n\nSo, of the given angles, the coterminal angles are $600^\\circ$ and $-480^\\circ$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059512 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d65819f-0528-527e-8d1a-20421e221ca5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Coterminal Angles in Radians"}] :question/id #uuid "5e722d3c-d592-591f-a6b6-faac38285630" :question/math-academy-id "e-3840" :question/problem "Which angle is coterminal with $\\dfrac {3 \\pi} {5}$?\n\n$\\dfrac{4\\pi}{5}, \\quad -\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}, \\quad -\\dfrac{11\\pi}{5}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta$ (in radians), we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi$.\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi$ to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {3 \\pi} {5}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3π}{5} + 2π = \\frac{13π}{5}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {13 \\pi} {5}\n$$\n\nso we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi$ from\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {3 \\pi} {5}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3π}{5} - 2π &= -\\frac{7π}{5}✓ \\\\\n\\frac{3π}{5} - 2 \\cdot (2π) &= -\\frac{17π}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac {17 \\pi} {5}\n$$\n\nso we can stop here.\n\nSo, of the given angles, the only coterminal angle is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059513 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059685 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "993c8fe4-dae9-5dc4-9f94-5012f905e0ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/id #uuid "1d570998-ebae-5dee-a9d7-8c439225a910" :question/math-academy-id "e-3841" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta = -435^\\circ$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-435^\\circ$ by *adding* integer multiples of $360^\\circ$ until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-435^{∘} + 360^{∘} &= -75^{∘} \\\\\n- 75^{∘} + 360^{∘} &= 285^{∘}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the angle $285^\\circ$ is coterminal with $-435^\\circ$. Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/4f/4f3fc79c2fc99478c407a63352d9113ef9cbef6a93a6feb62bd284d5f443bed5.png)\n\nWe see that $285^\\circ$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-435^\\circ$ also lies in this quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059514 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059686 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a119150-6d23-59bd-90c9-699de3a75795" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/id #uuid "ede68192-5d89-5204-8c49-a1eeebfbf48c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3842" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta = \\dfrac {8\\pi}3$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {8\\pi}3\n$$\n\nby *subtracting* integer multiples of $2\\pi$ until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0, 2\\pi)$.\n\n$$\n\\frac{8π}{3} - 2π = \\frac{2π}{3}✓\n$$\n\nSo the angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {2\\pi}3\n$$\n\nis coterminal with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {8\\pi}3\n$$\n\nLet's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/23/2360ed202deeca403c4631c319b69df30a9f0268b7ef270a8e436103c0ac3edb.png)\n\nWe see that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nlies in quadrant II. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nalso lies in this quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335733 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059685 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "993c8fe4-dae9-5dc4-9f94-5012f905e0ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fd1a287-023b-55c1-aa72-ee3fee24f836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333273} {:answer/id #uuid "29f2f53b-f6d3-57e8-8ad8-cd7e46ffff67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333381} {:answer/id #uuid "bd0f0f82-34f4-5ce0-8047-20cf96cbeda3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants III and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334632} {:answer/id #uuid "e97a56fa-9091-5cb5-bc68-c8e54a986827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335032} {:answer/id #uuid "f6fd1610-fe8c-5dc7-9d2a-2ac4b98636fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29f2f53b-f6d3-57e8-8ad8-cd7e46ffff67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333381} :answer-field/id #uuid "5593029c-b6f9-5d2e-b2b2-18177c24e6b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5324a1f-6f34-52eb-9030-0465e5ae23da" :question/math-academy-id "q-64675" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta =-{395}^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{395}^{∘}$ by *adding* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n- $-{395}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}=-{35}^{∘}$\n\n- $-{395}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={325}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${325}^{∘}$ is coterminal with $-{395}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/93/936e222cebb005bc504867f26eb2144bb976cab2441e53051121e056be158be1.png)\n\nWe see that ${325}^{∘}$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-{395}^{∘}$ also lies in this quadrant."}]}
